=== PRE-REGISTRATION (D3) -- is the per-session prompt_cache_key the lever?
H-CACHEKEY: with a CONSTANT prompt_cache_key across runs, the 27b reproduces 07-ttl-cache 3/3, because the pod's
prefix-cache routing (slot / cached-prefix chunking) is what perturbs a near-tie first token, not the sampler.
  D3  07-ttl-cache, 27b, temp 0, N=3, dumps on, ZBENCH_PIN_MODE=1 (constant session id 000..0, hence constant
      prompt_cache_key zakcode/000..0; the prompt carries no session id either way). zc-01, queued behind D2.
Predictions: 3/3 byte-identical AND the first assistant response identical across the three runs.
Decision rules:
  D3 holds  -> the lever is the cache key: ZAKCODE_STABLE_PROMPT_IDENTITY must also pin prompt_cache_key (a per-
               workspace key, e.g. zakcode/ws-<hash of workspace_root>), designed and shipped behind the same flag;
               then re-run the real-user cell on 07 and 08 with the fix (N=3 each) before claiming.
  D3 splits with call 1 byte-identical INCLUDING the key -> provider nondeterminism on identical wire input under
               long agentic prompts (prefix cache / batching numerics); the loop cannot remove it; the ADR records it
               as the boundary, with the environment-side timing line (run-1 vs run-3) as the second, separate source.
  Instrument refusal (rc 4/5) -> not a result.
D2 (the 35b on 07) reads independently: it says whether the 27b is special, not whether the key is the lever.

